  What this is, in plain words.  The switch sent an SNMPv2c notification from its own address,
  10.38.22.34, to the configured trap host.  The notification says which sensor is in trouble,
  by name, with the temperature it read and the limit it crossed.  That is the trap this case
  is named after.  It was caught on the wire.  It was not inferred from a log line.

  Two notification OIDs, and what each one means here.  Neither is one of the standard
  notifications that `snmpwire.py` can name, so no name is claimed for them.  What IS measured
  is which one appears when:

    snmpTrapOID.0 value                      observed when                  upper-limit varbind
    1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.5           the sensor went out of limits   20 (the lowered limit)
    1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.6           the sensor came back in limits  61 (the restored limit)

  Both sit directly under the AT-ENVMONv2 subtree 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12, and they carry
  that subtree's temperature table columns as varbinds.
  The fault and the clear can be told apart ONLY by the value of `snmpTrapOID.0`.  The five
  identity varbinds are byte-identical in both, and even the reading is the same 27.  A manager
  that looks only at the varbind OIDs cannot tell "too hot" from "recovered".

  The times line up on both clocks:

    event             DUT log (UTC)   DUT + 15 s = box    trap on the wire (box)
    alarm asserted    02:51:17        15:51:32            15:51:31.396
    alarm cleared     02:52:47        15:53:02            15:53:01.597
  => STEP 5 PASS: the over-temperature trap was produced and captured on the wire.  It names
     the sensor, carries the reading 27 and carries the crossed limit 20.  The matching clear
     trap arrived on restore.  STEP 3 captured 0 packets with the same instrument, so the 2
     packets here are a measurement.

