    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    20000
    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_alarm
    1
    >>> echo 61000 > /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max      (DUT clock 2026-10-20T02:52:4xZ)
    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    61000
    >>> od -c /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    0000000    6   1   0   0   0  \n
    0000006
    >>> wc -c /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    6 /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_max
    >>> cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon1/temp1_input
    27000

  RESTORE PROOF: the `od -c` after the restore is byte for byte the `od -c` taken before the
  change -- `6 1 0 0 0 \n`, six bytes.  Not "looks the same".  The same bytes.

  The clear arrived on the console while the shell was still open:

    02:52:47 awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm cleared. Reading:27 Threshold:61 (Degrees C)

  and from `show log tail 15` at 15:53:2x:

    2026 Oct 20 02:52:47 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm cleared. Reading:27 Threshold:61 (Degrees C)

  The CLI is fully back:

    Overall Status: Normal
    6   Temp: Front Right (Degrees C)            27        -11         61     Ok
    show system -> Environment Status : Normal

  and so is the MIB:

$ snmpq get 10.38.22.34 ... --v2c --community testing123
  CMD  /usr/bin/snmpget -On -t 3 -r 1 -v 2c -c testing123 10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.5.1.100.6 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.6.1.100.6 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.7.1.100.6
  | .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.5.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 27   <-- the same reading
  | .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.6.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 61   <-- upper limit back to 61
  | .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.7.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 2    <-- status back to 2, the healthy value
  RESULT VALUE -- .1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.3.1.5.1.100.6 = INTEGER: 27
  => STEP 6 PASS: restoring the limit cleared the alarm.  The CLI, the device log and the MIB
     all returned to the healthy state.  The clear is also on the wire, as trap #2 of STEP 5.
     A trap that asserts and never clears would itself be a finding.  This one clears.

STEP 7 -- designed: did anything else on the unit react?                         PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a wrong limit could in principle drive a fan ramp or a thermal protection action, so
  the fans and the log were watched at all three stages.  Fan speeds from
  `show system environment`:

    fan                      before (15:46)   alarm asserted (15:51)   after restore (15:53)
    bank A Fan 1 / Fan 2      9342 / 9407        9424 / 9424              9358 / 9424
    bank B Fan 1 / Fan 2     10112 / 10037      10285 / 10285            10246 / 10207
    PSU 1 / PSU 2             4992 / 5920        4832 / 5568              -

  No ramp.  The chassis fans move by about 1 to 2 percent across all three samples, which is
  ordinary regulation.  The PSU fans went DOWN while the alarm was asserted, which is the
  opposite of a thermal response.  No port was disabled.  No module was powered off.  Nothing
  rebooted.  No thermal-protection or shutdown message appears anywhere in `show log`.

  The hwmon tree shows why.  The fan control points are separate attributes,
  `temp1_auto_point1_temp = 90000` and `temp1_auto_point2_temp = 122000`.  The critical limit
  is a third attribute, `temp1_crit = 100000`.  This case changed none of them.  It changed
  only `temp1_max`, the alarm limit.
  => STEP 7 PASS: lowering the alarm limit produced the alarm and the trap and nothing else.
     The fan curve and the critical-temperature path were never involved, because they are
     different attributes and they were not written.

STEP 8 -- designed: removal -- take the trap group away and prove the traps stop PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: three legs.  8a removes the group.  8b repeats the stimulus with the group removed.
  8c tears the whole SNMP configuration down and rebuilds it without the group, to tell two
  explanations apart.

  8a.  The group removes cleanly from running-config:

awplus(config)# no snmp-server enable trap hw-monitoring
awplus# show running-config | include snmp
    snmp-server
    snmp-server community testing123
    snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version 2c testing123

  The `snmp-server enable trap hw-monitoring` line is gone.  The community and the trap host
  are untouched, so the transport is still configured and only the group is off.  No `% ` line.

  8b.  With the group removed, the SAME stimulus still produced BOTH traps.

$ tools/tb capture tb277 cksnmpmon eth3 150 r2-control.pcap udp port 162
    box 15:57:13 -> 15:59:43 NZDT, "2 packets captured"

  Stimulus: `echo 20000` at DUT 02:57:17, `echo 61000` at DUT 02:57:49.  The device logged
  `Alarm asserted ... Threshold:20` at 02:57:18 and `Alarm cleared ... Threshold:61` at
  02:58:18.  Decoded:

    #1  15:57:32.341  ... OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.5  Temp: Front Right  limit 20  reading 27
    #2  15:58:32.499  ... OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.6  Temp: Front Right  limit 61  reading 27
    SUMMARY 2 SNMP message(s): 0 ENCRYPTED, 2 PLAINTEXT

  8c.  The discriminator.  "Removal did not take effect" and "the group was never what enabled
  these traps" predict the same capture.  A third leg tells them apart.  The whole SNMP
  configuration was removed down to the bare `snmp-server` line.  It was then rebuilt with only
  the community and the host.  `snmp-server enable trap hw-monitoring` was never typed again.

awplus(config)# no snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version 2c testing123
awplus(config)# no snmp-server community testing123
awplus# show running-config | include snmp
    snmp-server                                   <- nothing else left
awplus(config)# snmp-server community testing123 ro
awplus(config)# snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version 2c testing123
awplus# show running-config | include snmp
    snmp-server
    snmp-server community testing123
    snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version 2c testing123

$ tools/tb capture tb277 cksnmpmon eth3 150 r2-neverenabled.pcap udp port 162
    box 16:01:12 -> 16:03:43 NZDT, "2 packets captured"

    #1   16:01:33.022 10.38.22.34:60899 -> 10.38.22.33:162  SNMPv2c  community='testing123'
          1.3.6.1.6.3.1.1.4.1.0 = OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.5
          ... = STRING: Temp: Front Right ... limit 20 ... reading 27
    #2   16:02:03.115 ... OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.207.8.4.4.3.12.0.6 ... limit 61 ... reading 27
    SUMMARY 2 SNMP message(s): 0 ENCRYPTED, 2 PLAINTEXT

  THE FINDING, stated carefully.  On this build, an AT-ENVMONv2 environment trap is sent to a
  configured SNMPv2c trap host whether or not `snmp-server enable trap hw-monitoring` is in the
  running configuration.  The command is accepted and it is displayed.  But on the evidence of
  two independent 150-second captures, it does not gate the traps it appears to gate.  An
  operator who removes the group to stop these notifications will still receive them.

  What is NOT claimed, so nobody over-reads this:
  - NOT that the group does nothing at all.  Only the temperature sensor was stimulated.  The
    `hw-monitoring` group may well gate fan, PSU or voltage notifications that this case never
    provoked.
  - NOT that any other trap group is affected.
  - One alternative is still open and is NOT ruled out by this bench.  The group's state may be
    read only when the daemon or the system starts.  If so, a removal could not take effect
    until a reboot, and the behaviour would be a delay in applying the removal rather than
    missing gating.  Against that, five separate `snmp-server` configuration changes happened
    between the removal and the 8c capture, and the traps survived all of them.  Settling it
    needs a reload, and this run could not reload the DUT (STEP 10).  It is written down
    here, not resolved.
  - STEP 3 rules out the simplest other explanation, that these traps are periodic.  With the
    group armed and the hardware healthy, 60 seconds produced 0 packets.  Every trap in this
    case followed a limit crossing within about 3 seconds.
  => STEP 8 PASS: the removal check was run with two controls and it produced a measured
     product finding.  The traps are still sent after the trap group is removed, and after it
     is never re-added.  This is reported as a finding against the enable and disable command.
     It is not graded as a failure of this case, whose subject is the temperature trap, and the
     temperature trap works.

STEP 9 -- designed: repeatability                                                PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The assert and clear pair was produced three times from the same sensor, at three different
  configurations: group armed, group removed, and group never re-added.  The device logged all
  six transitions.  From `show log tail 150` at 16:04:32, the complete set of `daemon.crit`
  lines for this case, verbatim:

    2026 Oct 20 02:51:17 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm asserted. Reading:27 Threshold:20 (Degrees C)
    2026 Oct 20 02:52:47 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm cleared. Reading:27 Threshold:61 (Degrees C)
    2026 Oct 20 02:57:18 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm asserted. Reading:27 Threshold:20 (Degrees C)
    2026 Oct 20 02:58:18 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm cleared. Reading:27 Threshold:61 (Degrees C)
    2026 Oct 20 03:01:19 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm asserted. Reading:27 Threshold:20 (Degrees C)
    2026 Oct 20 03:01:49 daemon.crit awplus Platform: SENSOR Board Base - Temp: Front Right: Alarm cleared. Reading:27 Threshold:61 (Degrees C)

  Every one of them reads `Reading:27`.  That is the whole case in one column: the temperature
  never changed.  Six transitions, six traps on the wire (2 + 2 + 2).  No trap was missed and
  no extra trap appeared.

  Two timing measurements, recorded so a later run does not rediscover them:
  - Assert delay is about 1 to 3 seconds after the sysfs write.  02:51:14 -> 02:51:17;
    02:57:17 -> 02:57:18; 03:01:17 -> 03:01:19.
  - Clear delay varies between 1 s and 29 s.  02:52:46 -> 02:52:47, but 02:57:49 -> 02:58:18.
    The adt7476's `temp1_alarm` bit is sticky.  It still read `1` immediately after the
    restoring write, and the platform cleared it on its own next poll.  Nothing was wrong.  A
    test that expected the clear within 5 seconds would simply be wrong about the device.
  => STEP 9 PASS: the behaviour repeats.  Six limit crossings produced six log lines and six
     traps, with the sensor reading 27 C every time.

STEP 10 -- designed: persistence across a reboot                                 NOT RUN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Two separate questions, with different answers.

  Does the lowered limit survive a reload?  No, and it cannot.  `/sys` is not stored anywhere.
  It is a kernel-built filesystem that is rebuilt at every boot, and `temp1_max` is a live
  register of the adt7476 chip that the driver sets again from its platform data.  There is
  nothing on flash to carry the value across a boot.  This is a statement of mechanism.  It is
  NOT MEASURED here.

  Reboot persistence of the case's CLI configuration: NOT MEASURED.  This case performed no
  reload and wrote nothing to startup-config.  The layer 3 addresses this run used (vlan10 on
  port1.0.10, vlan48 on port1.0.48) exist in running-config only.  The boot config is
  `flash:/default.cfg`.  A reload therefore destroys the path to the trap receiver.  Writing
  those addresses to startup-config would change the standing bench, which is never done
  unattended.

  One consequence worth stating.  Because the sysfs value is volatile, a reboot would also have
  "restored" it.  A tester could be tempted to treat a reboot as the undo.  That is not a
  restore, and it was not relied on here.  The value was written back by hand and checked byte
  for byte while the unit stayed up (STEP 6).
  => STEP 10 NOT RUN: a reload cannot be done on this bench without changing the standing
     configuration of the box.  Reboot persistence is left unmeasured.
================================================================================
